 ],
 "literal": "Because you agreed, I brought him, and he came / and stood at the trysting place, the lord of the hills. / 'Let him go for today,' you say. / My hands and feet are exhausted and suffering, / trembling like a sprout touched by fire, / there is nothing at all for me to do.",
 "translation": "Because you agreed, I brought him here, and the lord of the hills came and stood at the trysting place. But now you say, 'Let him go back for today.' My hands and feet are exhausted and distressed; trembling like a tender sprout touched by fire, there is absolutely nothing I can do.",
 "modern": "நீ சம்மதித்ததால் நான் அழைத்து வர, குன்றுகளின் தலைவன் வந்து சந்திக்கும் இடத்தில் காத்து நிற்கிறான். ஆனால் நீயோ, 'இன்றைக்குப் போதும், அவர் போகட்டும்' என்று சொல்கிறாய். என் கைகளும் கால்களும் சோர்ந்து வருந்த, நெருப்புப் பட்ட தளிரைப் போல நடுங்கி, நான் செய்வதற்கு என்று இனி ஒன்றுமே இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) is speaking to the heroine (talaivi) in this Pālai (wasteland/separation) poem. The friend had arranged a secret meeting (tryst) with the hero after getting the heroine's consent. However, when the hero arrives and waits at the designated spot, the heroine hesitates or refuses to meet him, telling the friend to send him away for the day. The friend expresses her deep distress and helplessness at this sudden change of mind. She describes her physical state as trembling like a delicate sprout caught in fire, showing how painful it is for her to turn away the waiting lover. The key words are 'குறி' (trysting place), 'சென்றைக்கு' (for going), and 'தீயுறு தளிர்' (sprout touched by fire).",
